NNAAMMEE
     llaassttccoommmm — show last commands executed

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     llaassttccoommmm [--EESScceessuu] [--ff _f_i_l_e] [++_f_o_r_m_a_t] [_c_o_m_m_a_n_d _._._.] [_u_s_e_r _._._.]
              [_t_e_r_m_i_n_a_l _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The llaassttccoommmm utility gives information on previously executed commands.
     With no arguments, llaassttccoommmm prints information about all the commands
     recorded during the current accounting file's lifetime.

     The following options are available:

     --EE      Print the time the process exited.

     --SS      Print the time the process started.

     --cc      Print the amount of cpu time used by the process.

     --ee      Print the amount of elapsed time used by the process.

     --ss      Print the amount of system time used by the process.

     --uu      Print the amount of user time used by the process.

     --ff _f_i_l_e
             Read from _f_i_l_e rather than the default _/_v_a_r_/_a_c_c_o_u_n_t_/_a_c_c_t.  If
             _f_i_l_e is a single dash (‘-’) llaassttccoommmm reads accounting entries
             from the standard input.

     An operand with a leading plus sign (‘+’) is followed a user-defined for‐
     mat string which specifies the format in which to display the process's
     start or exit date and time.  The format string may contain any of the
     conversion specifications described in the strftime(3) manual page, as
     well as arbitrary text.

     If no options are specified, --ccSS is assumed.  If llaassttccoommmm is invoked with
     arguments, only accounting entries with a matching _c_o_m_m_a_n_d name, _u_s_e_r
     name, or _t_e_r_m_i_n_a_l name are printed.  For example:

           lastcomm a.out root ttyd0

     would produce a listing of all the executions of commands named _a_._o_u_t by
     user _r_o_o_t on the terminal _t_t_y_d_0.

     For each process entry, the following are printed.

           ••   The name of the user who ran the process.
           ••   Flags, as accumulated by the accounting facilities in the sys‐
               tem.
           ••   The command name under which the process was called.
           ••   The amount of CPU (--cc), wall (--ee), system (--ss), or user (--uu)
               time used by the process (in seconds).
           ••   The time the process started (--SS) or exited (--EE).

     The flags are encoded as follows: ``S'' indicates the command was exe‐
     cuted by the super-user, ``F'' indicates the command ran after a fork,
     but without a following exec(3), ``D'' indicates the command terminated
     with the generation of a _c_o_r_e file, and ``X'' indicates the command was
     terminated with a signal.

     By default, accounting entries are printed going backwards in time,
     starting from the time llaassttccoommmm was executed.  However, if llaassttccoommmm reads
     entries from its standard input, then entries are printed in the order
     they are read.

FFIILLEESS
     /var/account/acct  default accounting file

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     The command
           lastcomm -Ee
     will print the exit time and elapsed time of each command logged in
     _/_v_a_r_/_a_c_c_o_u_n_t_/_a_c_c_t, while
           tail -f -c 0 /var/account/acct | lastcomm -f -
     will print details of each terminating command.

